A minimal way to make defense in depth concrete in code: chain the layers as ordinary functions and short-circuit on the first one that fails, so no single layer has to be perfect on its own.

```python
def input_filter(request):
    """Layer 1: catch known injection/adversarial patterns."""
    return not looks_like_known_attack_pattern(request.text)

def permission_check(request):
    """Layer 2: is this action even allowed for this caller, regardless
    of what the model decides to do?"""
    return request.action in ALLOWED_ACTIONS[request.caller_role]

def safety_classifier(request):
    """Layer 3: model-level safety signal on the actual response."""
    return safety_score(request.proposed_response) < RISK_THRESHOLD

DEFENSE_LAYERS = [input_filter, permission_check, safety_classifier]

def run_defense_in_depth(request):
    """Short-circuit on the first layer that fails -- no single layer
    needs to be perfect for the chain as a whole to hold."""
    for layer in DEFENSE_LAYERS:
        if not layer(request):
            return False, layer.__name__
    return True, None

allowed, failed_layer = run_defense_in_depth(incoming_request)
if not allowed:
    log_blocked_request(incoming_request, failed_layer)
```

Even if `input_filter` misses a novel attack pattern, the same request still has to clear `permission_check` and `safety_classifier` — one bypassed layer doesn't mean the request goes through unchecked.
